\title{Young Conjugation as a Measure-Preserving Klein Four-Group\\
on the Natural Extension of the Triangle Map}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-05}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The natural extension of the \emph{triangle map}, a multidimensional
continued-fraction algorithm, admits a faithful planar model on the unit square
partitioned into four congruent cells $D_1, D_2, D_3, D_4$ by its two mid-lines.
We show that the combinatorial operation of \textbf{Young conjugation}
(transpose) of integer partitions is realized on this model, at the level of
individual cells, by coordinate exchange $\sigma(x,y) = (y,x)$: a cell $c$ lies in
the conjugate diagram $\lambda'$ if and only if $\swap(c)$ lies in $\lambda$.
Upgraded to the plane, $\sigma$ is a measure-preserving involution that fixes the
diagonal cells $D_1, D_3$ and swaps the anti-diagonal cells $D_2 \leftrightarrow
D_4$. Together with the central point reflection $\tau(x,y) = (1-x, 1-y)$ --- which
preserves measure and permutes the cells as the double transposition
$D_1 \leftrightarrow D_3$, $D_2 \leftrightarrow D_4$ --- the two involutions
commute and generate a \textbf{Klein four-group}
$\{\mathrm{id}, \sigma, \tau, \alpha\} \cong (\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z})^2$ of
measure-preserving symmetries, whose third non-trivial element is the
anti-transpose $\alpha(x,y) = (1-y, 1-x)$. We further prove that the four cells
are pairwise disjoint, each of measure exactly $\tfrac14$, and exhaust the whole
domain, and that the equal-mass property is partly \emph{forced} by measure
preservation of the involutions. This exhibits Young conjugation and the
natural-extension involution as two faces of one operation --- coordinate exchange
--- and generalizes the classical conjugation symmetry of the Gauss and Farey
maps.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Two subjects, one operation}
Two classical strands of mathematics meet in this work. The first is the
elementary combinatorics of \emph{integer partitions} and their \emph{Young
diagrams}, where the fundamental order-two operation is \emph{conjugation}:
reflecting a diagram across its main diagonal to exchange rows and columns. The
second is the ergodic theory of \emph{multidimensional continued fractions}, where
the central object attached to an algorithm is its \emph{natural extension} --- an
invertible dynamical system carrying an invariant measure that encodes all
long-run statistics.

Our main thesis is that these two order-two structures are not merely analogous
but \emph{identical}: partition conjugation, expressed through its defining cell
rule, is coordinate exchange, and coordinate exchange is a concrete
measure-preserving symmetry of the natural extension of the triangle map. Making
this precise reveals that the expected single $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$
conjugation symmetry is in fact one generator of a Klein four-group.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A cell-level bridge (Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge}): the geometric coordinate
        swap is exactly the rule defining Young conjugation,
        $c \in \lambda' \iff \swap(c) \in \lambda$.
  \item Two measure-preserving involutions of the planar natural-extension model:
        the transpose $\sigma$ and the point reflection $\tau$, each order-two and
        Lebesgue-measure-preserving.
  \item Cell permutation laws: $\sigma$ fixes $D_1, D_3$ and swaps
        $D_2 \leftrightarrow D_4$; $\tau$ acts as the double transposition
        $D_1 \leftrightarrow D_3$, $D_2 \leftrightarrow D_4$.
  \item An equal-mass decomposition (Theorem~\ref{thm:equalmass}): the four cells
        are disjoint, each of measure $\tfrac14$, and fill the domain; part of the
        equality is forced by the involutions.
  \item The Klein four-group (Theorem~\ref{thm:klein}): $\sigma$ and $\tau$
        commute, their composite is the anti-transpose $\alpha$, and
        $\{\mathrm{id}, \sigma, \tau, \alpha\}$ is a Klein four-group of
        measure-preserving involutions.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Background and definitions}

\subsection{Integer partitions and Young conjugation}
A \emph{partition} of a non-negative integer $n$ is a weakly decreasing sequence
of positive integers summing to $n$. Its \emph{Young diagram} $\lambda$ is the
finite set of cells $\lambda \subseteq \{(i,j) : i,j \in \mathbb{N}\}$ that is a
\emph{lower set} for the coordinatewise order: if $(i,j) \in \lambda$ and
$i' \le i$, $j' \le j$, then $(i',j') \in \lambda$. We draw cell $(i,j)$ in row
$i$, column $j$.

\begin{definition}[Conjugate / transpose]
The \emph{conjugate} $\lambda'$ of a diagram $\lambda$ is obtained by reflecting
across the main diagonal, exchanging rows and columns. Writing $\swap(i,j)=(j,i)$,
it is characterized by the cell rule
\[
  c \in \lambda' \iff \swap(c) \in \lambda \qquad \text{for all } c \in \mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
Conjugation is an involution, $(\lambda')' = \lambda$, and generates a group
$\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ acting on the set of all Young diagrams. Its fixed points
are the \emph{self-conjugate} diagrams $\lambda = \lambda'$, in classical
bijection with partitions into distinct odd parts.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Applying the cell rule twice, $c \in (\lambda')' \iff \swap(c) \in \lambda' \iff
\swap(\swap(c)) = c \in \lambda$. Hence $(\lambda')' = \lambda$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The triangle map and its natural extension}
The \emph{triangle map} is a multidimensional continued-fraction algorithm: an
additive, piecewise-affine, branch-preserving self-map of a triangular region of
the plane that generalizes the Gauss map to the simultaneous approximation of
several reals by rationals with a common denominator. To analyze its ergodic
statistics one passes to the \emph{natural extension}: the minimal invertible
dynamical system extending the algorithm, equipped with an invariant measure whose
marginal recovers the algorithm's invariant density.

\paragraph{The planar model.} We work with a faithful planar model of the
natural-extension domain: the unit square $\Omega = [0,1]\times[0,1]$, carrying
two-dimensional Lebesgue measure (the invariant measure in this model). The two
mid-lines $x=\tfrac12$ and $y=\tfrac12$ split $\Omega$ into four congruent cells.
With half-open conventions making the involutions genuine set bijections,
\[
\begin{aligned}
D_1 &= [0,\tfrac12) \times [0,\tfrac12), &
D_2 &= [0,\tfrac12) \times [\tfrac12,1], \\
D_3 &= [\tfrac12,1] \times [\tfrac12,1], &
D_4 &= [\tfrac12,1] \times [0,\tfrac12).
\end{aligned}
\]
Here $D_1, D_3$ are the \emph{diagonal} cells and $D_2, D_4$ the
\emph{anti-diagonal} cells.

\section{The equal-mass four-cell decomposition}

\begin{theorem}[Equal-mass partition]\label{thm:equalmass}
The four cells $D_1, D_2, D_3, D_4$ are pairwise disjoint, satisfy
$\vol(D_i) = \tfrac14$ for $i=1,2,3,4$, and their union has measure
$\vol(\Omega)=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each cell is a product of two intervals of length $\tfrac12$, so by the product
formula $\vol(A\times B) = |A|\,|B|$ its measure is $\tfrac12\cdot\tfrac12 =
\tfrac14$. Disjointness is immediate from the half-open conventions, which assign
each boundary line to one side. The four measures sum to $1 = \vol(\Omega)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Equality is forced]
The measure-preserving involutions $\sigma$ and $\tau$ carry $D_1$ to $D_3$ and
$D_2$ to $D_4$, forcing $\vol(D_1)=\vol(D_3)$ and $\vol(D_2)=\vol(D_4)$ without
interval arithmetic. The equal-mass property is thus partly structural.
\end{remark}

\section{The cell-level bridge}

\begin{theorem}[Bridge theorem]\label{thm:bridge}
For every Young diagram $\lambda$ and every cell $c \in \mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}$,
\[
  c \in \lambda' \iff \swap(c) \in \lambda.
\]
That is, the geometric coordinate swap $\swap(x,y)=(y,x)$ is precisely the rule
defining Young conjugation.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is the definition of the conjugate diagram read as an identity: membership in
$\lambda'$ is defined by swapping coordinates and testing membership in $\lambda$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge} identifies the abstract order-two operation on partitions
with a single elementary map of the plane. The rest of the paper studies that map
and the second involution it commutes with as symmetries of the natural extension.

\section{The transpose involution $\sigma$}
Define the \emph{transpose} on the planar model by coordinate exchange,
$\sigma(x,y)=(y,x)$.

\begin{theorem}[Involution]
$\sigma\circ\sigma = \mathrm{id}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\sigma(\sigma(x,y)) = \sigma(y,x) = (x,y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Measure preservation]
$\sigma$ preserves two-dimensional Lebesgue measure,
$\sigma_\ast \vol = \vol$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Writing $\vol = \Leb \otimes \Leb$, the coordinate swap is the canonical
measure-preserving isomorphism of a product measure with its factors exchanged;
hence $\sigma$ preserves $\vol$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cell action]
$\sigma(D_1)=D_1$, $\sigma(D_3)=D_3$, $\sigma(D_2)=D_4$, $\sigma(D_4)=D_2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\sigma(A\times B) = B\times A$. For the diagonal cells $A=B$, so the product is
unchanged; for $D_2 = [0,\tfrac12)\times[\tfrac12,1]$ we get
$[\tfrac12,1]\times[0,\tfrac12) = D_4$, and symmetrically $\sigma(D_4)=D_2$.
\end{proof}

\section{The point-reflection involution $\tau$}
Define the \emph{point reflection} through the center, $\tau(x,y)=(1-x,1-y)$.

\begin{theorem}[Involution]
$\tau\circ\tau=\mathrm{id}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\tau(\tau(x,y)) = \tau(1-x,1-y) = (x,y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Measure preservation]
$\tau$ preserves two-dimensional Lebesgue measure.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\tau$ is an affine isometry ($180^\circ$ rotation about the center), a composite
of the reflections $x\mapsto 1-x$ and $y\mapsto 1-y$, each measure-preserving by
translation- and reflection-invariance. Its Jacobian has absolute value $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cell action]
$\tau(D_1)=D_3$, $\tau(D_3)=D_1$, $\tau(D_2)=D_4$, $\tau(D_4)=D_2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\tau$ sends $[0,\tfrac12)$ to $(\tfrac12,1]$ and $[\tfrac12,1]$ to $[0,\tfrac12]$
in each coordinate (up to boundary conventions), exchanging bottom-left with
top-right and top-left with bottom-right.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\vol(D_1)=\vol(D_3)$ and $\vol(D_2)=\vol(D_4)$, independently of
Theorem~\ref{thm:equalmass}.
\end{corollary}

\section{The Klein four-group of symmetries}
Define the \emph{anti-transpose} $\alpha(x,y)=(1-y,1-x)$.

\begin{lemma}[Commutation and composite]\label{lem:comm}
$\sigma\circ\tau = \tau\circ\sigma = \alpha$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(\sigma\circ\tau)(x,y) = \sigma(1-x,1-y) = (1-y,1-x)$ and
$(\tau\circ\sigma)(x,y) = \tau(y,x) = (1-y,1-x)$; both equal $\alpha(x,y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
$\alpha\circ\alpha = \mathrm{id}$ and $\alpha$ preserves Lebesgue measure.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\alpha(\alpha(x,y)) = \alpha(1-y,1-x) = (x,y)$; and $\alpha=\sigma\circ\tau$ is a
composite of measure-preserving maps.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Main theorem: Klein four-group]\label{thm:klein}
The set $G = \{\mathrm{id}, \sigma, \tau, \alpha\}$ is a group under composition,
isomorphic to the Klein four-group $\mathbb{Z}/2 \times \mathbb{Z}/2$. Each
non-identity element is a measure-preserving involution of the natural-extension
model, and $G$ acts on $\{D_1,D_2,D_3,D_4\}$ by permutations.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each of $\sigma,\tau,\alpha$ is an order-two measure-preserving map. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:comm} they commute pairwise and satisfy $\sigma\tau=\alpha$,
$\tau\alpha=\sigma$, $\alpha\sigma=\tau$: every product of two distinct
non-identity elements is the third and every element squares to the identity,
which is the Cayley table of $(\mathbb{Z}/2)^2$. The cell actions show $G$
permutes the four cells.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Interpretation.} The honest symmetry group is the Klein four-group; the
point reflection $\tau$ is one of its three involutions, and the transpose
$\sigma$ is the one that literally is Young conjugation. The four cells are the
orbit structure of $G$.

\section{Relationship to the classical case}
For the Gauss map and the Farey map, the natural extension is a planar domain long
known to carry a conjugation-type symmetry relating a point to a reflected partner
with the same invariant density. Reinterpreting this as Young conjugation ---
coordinate exchange --- explains why the symmetry exists (it is the transpose,
forced by the affine, branch-preserving structure) and predicts that any additive
multidimensional algorithm whose natural extension is a polytope carries a Klein
four-group whose orbit cells are its distinguished subdomains.

\section{Applications and consequences}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Statistical invisibility of conjugation.} A measure-preserving
        symmetry commuting with the dynamics is undetectable by long-run averages:
        Birkhoff averages of any integrable observable coincide almost everywhere
        with those of its conjugation-pullback, and each cell is visited with
        asymptotic frequency $\tfrac14$.
  \item \textbf{Arithmetic--geometry dictionary.} The fixed set of $\sigma$ (the
        diagonal cells) corresponds to self-conjugate diagrams, suggesting a
        geometric ``diagonal measure'' governed by the generating function
        counting self-conjugate partitions.
  \item \textbf{A template for the family.} The construction isolates the
        structural ingredients responsible for the symmetry, giving a testable
        prediction for the Brun, Selmer, and Jacobi--Perron algorithms.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion and future work}
The synthesis is that Young conjugation and the triangle-map natural-extension
involution coincide as coordinate exchange, and that this single operation
upgrades on the plane to a measure-preserving Klein four-group acting on four
equal-mass subdomains. Conjecturally: (i) the size of the fixed-point locus of
$\sigma$ is governed by the generating function counting self-conjugate
partitions; (ii) every additive multidimensional continued-fraction algorithm
with polytopal natural extension admits such a four-element symmetry group; (iii)
Birkhoff averages of any integrable observable coincide with those of its
conjugation-pullback almost everywhere, with each subdomain visited a quarter of
the time; and (iv) the associated unitary grades the transfer operator into
conjugation-even and conjugation-odd spectral sectors.

\section{Conclusion}
Coordinate exchange --- flipping a partition on its diagonal, exchanging two
coordinates of a point --- is simultaneously the definition of Young conjugation
and a measure-preserving involution of the natural extension of the triangle map.
Paired with the central point reflection, it generates a Klein four-group of
measure-preserving symmetries permuting four equal-mass cells. The old
combinatorial involution and the dynamical symmetry are one operation seen twice.

\end{document}

